+## ⬛ DECISION NEEDED — W16 is now enforced, and it makes T-7 unclaimable
+
+**What changed.** `.claude/scripts/harness_lib.py` now refuses, at claim AND at
+close, any ticket whose verify decides PASS/FAIL by reading the CONTENT of a
+data/config file that the same ticket's own scope lets it write. Measured across
+the whole plan: **1 ticket flagged (T-7), 31 clean.** Audit it yourself with
+
+```
+python3 .claude/scripts/harness_lib.py selfgate
+```
+
+**Why it fires on T-7.** T-7's verify is
+`python -c "... open('evals/labeled_set.yaml')['approval'] ... sys.exit(0 if
+status=='APPROVED' and approved_by and approved_date else 1)"`, and T-7's scope is
+`['evals/**', ...]`. The gate reads exactly the three fields the ticket is
+authorised to write. This is true **regardless of whether your sign-off was
+genuine** — the point is that the gate cannot tell, so the receipt attests
+nothing. A fingerprint does not help: it faithfully attests whatever bytes are
+there.
+
+**What this costs you.** T-7 already has a receipt, and this change does NOT
+revoke it. But T-7 can no longer be re-claimed or re-closed — so if the approval
+is ever reverted and T-7 needs re-doing honestly, it is blocked until the plan is
+amended. `docs/tickets.json` is PROTECTED and only T-14/T-15/T-26 name it in
+scope, so **this is your call, not a session's.** Three options:
+
+1. **Point the gate at ground truth outside T-7's scope** — e.g. have it require
+   a signature in `evals/REVIEW.md` (which is in T-15's scope, not T-7's) and
+   drop the `labeled_set.yaml` read. Best: it fixes the loop rather than hiding
+   it, and it is the same direction the earlier "W16-structural" note proposed.
+2. **Remove `evals/labeled_set.yaml` from T-7's write authority** (narrow
+   `evals/**` to the files T-7 legitimately authors). Also closes the loop; more
+   fiddly to express.
+3. **Amend nothing and leave T-7 closed as-is.** The check then simply prevents
+   any *future* ticket from taking the same shape. Nothing breaks today.
+
+**Known limits, so you are not over-trusting this.** It reads the verify STRING
+only. (a) An existence-only gate (`test -f evals/approved.flag`) is deliberately
+NOT refused — refusing it broke 16 existing tests and would make every honest
+"produce this artifact" ticket unclaimable. (b) Indirection is invisible: a
+verify spelled `pytest backend/tests/evals` whose tests then read
+`labeled_set.yaml` is the same closed loop and cannot be seen from the string —
+that is precisely the shape the T-7 guard test took when it was re-expressed as a
+signature-FORMAT check, and no string rule will ever catch it. (c) Computed or
+globbed paths are not matched.
+
+---
+
