+"""W16: a ticket must not be able to verify itself against an artifact its own
+scope lets it write.
+
+THE DEFECT THIS BINDS. T-7's verify command is
+
+    uv run python -c "import yaml; a=yaml.safe_load(open('evals/labeled_set.yaml'))
+    ['approval']; import sys; sys.exit(0 if a.get('status')=='APPROVED' and
+    a.get('approved_by') and a.get('approved_date') else 1)" && ...
+
+and T-7's scope is ``['evals/**', 'backend/tests/evals/**',
+'backend/src/escalation/**']``. ``evals/labeled_set.yaml`` is inside ``evals/**``.
+So the gate that decides whether T-7 may close reads three fields that T-7 itself
+is authorised to write, and nothing else -- the receipt certifies a fact its own
+subject authored. Measured live before this check existed: a synthetic ticket of
+exactly that shape claimed cleanly, the agent flipped ``status:
+PROPOSED_AWAITING_HUMAN_REVIEW`` to ``status: APPROVED``, and ``claim.sh close``
+minted a fingerprint-bound receipt (rc=0). The fingerprint is not a defence here:
+it faithfully attests the forged bytes.
+
+WHY THE EXISTING SELF-GATING RULE MISSES IT. ``backend/tests/plan/_planlib.py``
+(T-14 acceptance 2) already forbids a ticket from invoking a SCRIPT it authors:
+it shlex-tokenises the verify string and asks whether any TOKEN is a repo-relative
+path matching the ticket's own scope. T-7's path lives INSIDE a ``python -c``
+program, which shlex hands back as one token that merely contains the substring --
+so ``find_self_gating_violations(T-7)`` returns ``[]`` for the one ticket in the
+plan that self-gates. ``harness_lib.self_gating_data_reads`` scans the raw verify
+string instead, so the path is found wherever it is written.
+
+PRECISION IS THE WHOLE PROBLEM. Nearly every verify in the plan runs pytest over
+test files that ARE in scope, and that is legitimate -- a ticket authoring the
+SUITE that gates it is ordinary TDD. Three conditions must hold together before
+anything is refused: the verify names a literal repo path (1), whose extension
+makes it data/config rather than executable test code (2), and which the ticket's
+own scope globs let it write (3). The table in
+``test_precision_over_verify_shapes`` is the evidence for each pair-wise
+near-miss, and ``test_no_new_self_verifying_ticket_enters_the_plan`` is the
+whole-plan measurement: across the real 32 tickets exactly one is flagged.
+
+KNOWN LIMITS, stated rather than papered over:
+  * EXISTENCE IS NOT CONTENT. ``test -f src/feature.txt`` under a ``src/**``
+    scope is not refused. Measured: refusing it broke 15 tests in
+    ``test_claim_ledger_integrity.py`` (plus one cascade in
+    ``test_skip_db_tests_relocation.py``) whose synthetic ticket uses exactly
+    that placeholder verify, and it would make every honest "produce this
+    artifact" ticket unclaimable. So an existence-only self-gate remains
+    possible; widening to it is a human judgement call, not a silent change.
+  * INDIRECTION IS INVISIBLE. This reads the verify STRING. A gate spelled
+    ``pytest backend/tests/evals`` whose tests then read
+    ``evals/labeled_set.yaml`` is a closed loop this cannot see -- and that is
+    the shape the T-7 remediation actually took when
+    ``test_labels_are_not_self_approved`` was re-expressed as a signature-format
+    check. Nothing here substitutes for reading what a guard test asserts.
+  * GLOBBED AND COMPUTED PATHS. ``glob('evals/*.yaml')`` or a path assembled at
+    runtime is not a literal token and is not matched.
+"""
+
+from __future__ import annotations
+
+import importlib.util
+import json
+import os
+import shutil
+import subprocess
+import sys
+from collections.abc import Iterator
+from pathlib import Path
+from typing import Any
+
+import pytest
+
+REPO_ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[3]
+HARNESS_LIB = REPO_ROOT / ".claude" / "scripts" / "harness_lib.py"
+CLAIM_SH = REPO_ROOT / ".claude" / "scripts" / "claim.sh"
+GEN_TASKS = REPO_ROOT / ".claude" / "scripts" / "gen_tasks.py"
+REAL_TICKETS = json.loads((REPO_ROOT / "docs" / "tickets.json").read_text())["tickets"]
